PART 14 – PMC APPROVAL AND IMMUTABLE REVISION SYSTEM

57. Mandatory PMC Approval Lock

CRITICAL: Once PMC approves a register revision, nobody, including the original creator, can directly edit or delete that approved revision.

Register statuses:

- Draft.
- Submitted.
- Pending PMC Approval.
- Approved – Locked.
- Edit Permission Requested.
- Edit Permission Granted.
- Edit Permission Expired.
- Rejected.
- Reopened.
- Resubmitted.

58. Approval Workflow

1. Engineer creates register.
2. Engineer enters measurements and records.
3. Engineer submits.
4. Editing becomes disabled.
5. PMC reviews.
6. PMC approves or rejects.
7. Approved revision becomes immutable.
8. Corrections require a new permission request.

59. Controlled Reopening

When the creator opens an approved register:

- Disable Edit.
- Disable Delete.
- Show Approved – Locked.
- Show Request Edit Permission.

The creator submits:

- Reason.
- Proposed Correction.
- Supporting Evidence.
- Request Date and Time.

PMC can:

- Grant Permission.
- Reject.
- Set Expiry.
- Revoke Permission.
- View History.

60. Temporary Editing

When PMC grants permission:

- Record PMC User ID.
- Record Grant Time.
- Record Expiry.
- Record Exact Register and Revision.
- Authorize only the designated creator.
- Block all other users.
- Prevent creator self-authorization.

On expiry or revocation, editing must immediately stop.

61. New Revision and Fresh Approval

After correction:

1. Creator submits corrected register.
2. Permission is revoked immediately.
3. New revision is created.
4. Previous approved revision remains unchanged.
5. New revision enters Pending PMC Approval.
6. PMC reviews again.
7. PMC approves or rejects.
8. New approved revision becomes immutable.

Never overwrite an earlier approved revision.

62. Database-Level Enforcement

Enforce approval and editing rules through PostgreSQL RLS, protected RPC functions and transactional database operations.

A direct API update must also fail when permission is absent.

Never rely only on Android buttons or UI conditions.

